  "notes": [
    "The two planes are the shipped components: the engine, the SDP machinery, the control channel's framing, the raw-UDP ARQ session and the H.264 software encoder. The datagrams travel over real UDP sockets.",
    "The media plane's saturation is real: the receiving side enforces a token-bucket capacity and drops the datagrams that arrive when the bucket is empty, exactly as a bottleneck router's queue overflows.",
    "The control plane rides the G2 shape: a separate 'raw-udp-arq' stack with its own socket, its own ARQ session and its own 5-tuple. Its address travels in the session description ('a=x-nimrtc-control:'), so the two planes' endpoints are really distinct rather than merely asserted to be.",
    "The control producer, the media producer and the engine tick each run on their own thread. That is the point of G2, not a convenience: driving both planes from one loop would put the software H.264 encode on the control plane's critical path, and the measurement would report the encoder's latency rather than the transport's.",
    "The numbers are machine-dependent (the host's real scheduling jitter, the loopback's real RTT), so the committed artifact is one local sample, not a pass/fail threshold. What must hold on every machine are the invariants, and they are asserted by tools/benchmark_control_saturation.py and recorded in the artifact.",
    "The 'negative_control' scenario runs the same rig with the video path disabled: it proves that a passing saturated run is not an artefact of the video path having been switched off by accident, and that the control path delivers every message on an idle link too.",
    "The control producer's schedule is absolute: a late wake is absorbed by the next slot rather than by shifting every slot that follows. The obvious alternative \u2014 resetting the phase to 'now + period' \u2014 makes the measured period the wake error plus the configured period, so a 500 us wake error turns a 5 ms period into a 5.5 ms one and the control plane reports ~182 Hz instead of the profile's 200 Hz. On Windows the wait is a high-resolution waitable timer (CREATE_WAITABLE_TIMER_HIGH_RESOLUTION), because the default ~15.6 ms system tick would cap the producer at ~64 Hz and the measured rate would describe the host's timer rather than the transport.",
    "The rate the binary runs at and the rate the invariants are checked against are the same number, taken from the profile unless the command line overrides it. They cannot drift apart: the defaults are 'unset' rather than the profile's values, because a default that *equals* the profile's value cannot be told apart from an explicit override, and a profile change would then leave the binary on the old rate while the assertion moved to the new one.",
    "The binary reports whether its wait path had the high-resolution deadline ('high_resolution_wait'). The rate invariant is **skipped** when it did not: the measured rate is produced by a thread waiting on a deadline, so without the 100 ns deadline the run measures the host's timer rather than the transport, and a rate that missed the target would say nothing about the control plane. It is skipped rather than failed because it is *unknown*, not wrong. The verdict's 'skips' array lists every such property, so a green verdict cannot hide an unchecked one.",
    "Both scenarios are retried when the *host* was too busy to produce a usable measurement: the saturated one when the link was never brought to capacity, the negative control when the control plane missed the rate. The negative control's rate is the interesting case: it carries no video at all, so its control plane has nothing to compete with, and a rate below the target there can only be the host's scheduling - never the transport. Without the retry the benchmark would report a transport failure for what is really a busy machine."
  ]
